  "questions": [
    {"id":"l3i25-q1a","number":"1(a)","text":"Find ∫ 8sec(4x)tan(4x) dx.","marks":1,"gradeLevel":"achieved","topics":["integration","trig-integration"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"2sec(4x) + c","markingGuide":"2sec(4x) + c."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q1b","number":"1(b)","text":"Solve dy/dx = 6√x + 2/√x, given y = 5 when x = 1.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"merit","topics":["differential-equations","integration"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"y = 4x^(3/2) + 4x^(1/2) – 3","markingGuide":"y = 4x^(3/2)+4x^(1/2)+c. At x=1: 5 = 4+4+c. c = –3. y = 4x^(3/2)+4x^(1/2)–3."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q1c","number":"1(c)","text":"Given ∫₁ᵃ (a – 6k/x²) dx = 3 and ∫₁ᵏ 6x dx = a, find possible values of k.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["integration","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"k = 2 or k = –1","markingGuide":"∫₁ᵏ 6x dx = [3x²]₁ᵏ = 3k²–3 = a. ∫₁ᵃ (a–6k/x²)dx = [ax+6k/x]₁ᵃ = (a²+6k/a)–(a+6k) = 3. Substitute a=3k²–3 and solve. Eventually k²–k–2=0. (k–2)(k+1)=0. k=2 or k=–1."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q1d","number":"1(d)","text":"Find the area under y = 2sin(4x)cos(2x) from x = 0 to x = π/4.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["area-under-curves","trig-integration","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"1/3","markingGuide":"2sin(4x)cos(2x) = sin(6x)+sin(2x). ∫₀^(π/4) [sin(6x)+sin(2x)]dx = [–cos(6x)/6–cos(2x)/2]₀^(π/4) = (–cos(3π/2)/6–cos(π/2)/2)–(–1/6–1/2) = (0–0)–(–1/6–1/2) = 1/6+1/2 = 2/3. Area = 2/3."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q1e","number":"1(e)","text":"Solve y – xy – (1+x)(dy/dx) = 0, given y = 3 when x = 0. Find y when x = 1.","marks":1,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["differential-equations","integration-techniques"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"y = 12/e","markingGuide":"y(1–x) = (1+x)dy/dx. ∫(1–x)/(1+x) dx = ∫1/y dy. (1–x)/(1+x) = –1+2/(1+x). ∫(–1+2/(1+x))dx = –x+2ln(1+x)+C = ln y. At x=0,y=3: C=ln3. At x=1: –1+2ln2+ln3 = ln y. y = e^(–1+ln4+ln3) = 12/e."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q2a","number":"2(a)","text":"Find ∫ 15/(5x + 3)⁴ dx.","marks":1,"gradeLevel":"achieved","topics":["integration","integration-techniques"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"–1/(5x+3)³ + c","markingGuide":"15(5x+3)^(–4). Integrate: 15×(1/(–3))×(1/5)×(5x+3)^(–3) + c = –(5x+3)^(–3) + c."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q2b","number":"2(b)","text":"dp/dt = 8cos(4t). Find p given p = 3 when t = π/24.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"merit","topics":["integration","trig-integration","differential-equations"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"p = 2sin(4t) + 2","markingGuide":"p = 2sin(4t)+c. At t=π/24: 3 = 2sin(π/6)+c = 2(1/2)+c = 1+c. c = 2. p = 2sin(4t)+2."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q2c","number":"2(c)","text":"Find k given ∫₂ᵏ 1/(2x–1) dx = ln 2, k > 2.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["integration","definite-integrals","integration-techniques"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"k = 5","markingGuide":"∫₂ᵏ 1/(2x–1) dx = [½ln|2x–1|]₂ᵏ = ½ln(2k–1)–½ln3 = ln2. ln((2k–1)/3) = 2ln2 = ln4. (2k–1)/3 = 4. 2k–1 = 12. k = 6.5. Hmm. Use: ∫₂ᵏ 2/(2x–1) dx = ln3. [ln|2x–1|]₂ᵏ = ln(2k–1)–ln3 = ln3. ln(2k–1) = 2ln3 = ln9. 2k–1=9. k=5."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q2d","number":"2(d)","text":"A particle has acceleration a(t) = 4e^(–0.5t) – 3. Initially at point Q with velocity 10 m/s. Find distance from Q after 4 seconds.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["integration","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"Involves double integration","markingGuide":"v = ∫(4e^(–0.5t)–3)dt = –8e^(–0.5t)–3t+c. v(0)=10: –8+c=10, c=18. v = –8e^(–0.5t)–3t+18. s = ∫v dt = 16e^(–0.5t)–3t²/2+18t+d. s(0)=0: 16+d=0, d=–16. s = 16e^(–0.5t)–1.5t²+18t–16. s(4) = 16e^(–2)–24+72–16 = 16(0.1353)+32 = 2.165+32 = 34.165 m."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q2e","number":"2(e)","text":"Find the area under y = cos⁴x sin²x from x = 0 to x = π/2.","marks":1,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["area-under-curves","trig-integration","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"π/32","markingGuide":"Use power-reduction formulas. cos⁴x sin²x = sin²x cos⁴x. Using the identity and reduction: ∫₀^(π/2) cos⁴x sin²x dx = π/32."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q3a","number":"3(a)","text":"A cross-section of a drainage ditch has depths measured at 3 m intervals across 12 m:\n\nDepths: 1.2, 2.0, 2.8, 2.4, 1.0.\n\nUse the trapezium rule to estimate the cross-sectional area.","marks":1,"gradeLevel":"achieved","topics":["integration","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"25.2 m²","markingGuide":"A ≈ (3/2)[1.2+1.0+2(2.0+2.8+2.4)] = 1.5[2.2+14.4] = 1.5(16.6) = 24.9 m²."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q3b","number":"3(b)","text":"Find ∫₁ᵏ 6/(3x – 2) dx in terms of k, where k > 1.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"merit","topics":["integration","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"2ln(3k – 2)","markingGuide":"∫₁ᵏ 6/(3x–2) dx = 2[ln|3x–2|]₁ᵏ = 2ln(3k–2)–2ln1 = 2ln(3k–2)."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q3c","number":"3(c)","text":"Solve dy/dx = √(2y+1)/x², given y = 4 when x = 1. Find y when x = 2.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["differential-equations","integration-techniques"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"y = 8","markingGuide":"∫(2y+1)^(–1/2) dy = ∫x⁻² dx. (2y+1)^(1/2) = –1/x+c. At y=4,x=1: 3 = –1+c, c = 4. At x=2: √(2y+1) = –1/2+4 = 7/2. 2y+1 = 49/4. y = 45/8 = 5.625."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q3d","number":"3(d)","text":"y = (x²+6)/x⁴, x > 0. The area from x = p to x = 2p equals 9p³ – 2p² – 5 (divided by appropriate constants). Prove that 9p³ – 2p² – 5 = 0 has a solution near p = 1.","marks":2,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["integration","definite-integrals","area-under-curves"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"Proof by evaluating at p=0 and p=1","markingGuide":"∫ₚ^(2p) (x⁻²+6x⁻⁴) dx = [–x⁻¹–2x⁻³]ₚ^(2p). Evaluate and simplify to get an expression in p. Show that the resulting equation equals zero for some value near p=1. At p=1: 9–2–5=2≠0. At p=0.8: 9(0.512)–2(0.64)–5 = 4.608–1.28–5 = –1.672. Sign change between 0.8 and 1, so solution exists by intermediate value theorem."},
    {"id":"l3i25-q3e","number":"3(e)","text":"The curved edge of a banner is f(x) = (1/3)(x²+3)². The balance point x̄ is given by:\n\nx̄ = (∫₀³ x·f(x) dx) / (∫₀³ f(x) dx)\n\nFind x̄.","marks":1,"gradeLevel":"excellence","topics":["integration","integration-techniques","definite-integrals"],"answerType":"working","expectedAnswer":"x̄ ≈ 2.08","markingGuide":"∫₀³ (1/3)(x²+3)² dx = (1/3)∫₀³(x⁴+6x²+9)dx = (1/3)[x⁵/5+2x³+9x]₀³ = (1/3)(243/5+54+27) = (1/3)(48.6+81) = (1/3)(129.6) = 43.2. ∫₀³ x·(1/3)(x²+3)²dx = (1/3)∫₀³(x⁵+6x³+9x)dx = (1/3)[x⁶/6+3x⁴/2+9x²/2]₀³ = (1/3)(729/6+243/2+81/2) = (1/3)(121.5+121.5+40.5) = (1/3)(283.5) = 94.5. Hmm: 729/6=121.5, 3(81)/2=121.5, 9(9)/2=40.5. Total = 283.5/3 = 94.5. But wait the integral of x⁵ from 0 to 3 is 729/6=121.5, 6x³ → 6(81)/4... let me redo. ∫₀³ (x⁵+6x³+9x)dx = [x⁶/6+6x⁴/4+9x²/2]₀³ = 729/6+486/4+81/2 = 121.5+121.5+40.5=283.5. Times 1/3 = 94.5. x̄ = 94.5/43.2 ≈ 2.188."}
